-- title:
--   §C.1, $n=4$ — exchange performs $\mathfrak{su}(1)$, $\mathfrak{su}(3)$, $\mathfrak{su}(2)$ independently on the four-qubit DFSs
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be the real Lie algebra generated by the $iE_{ij}$, $i\neq j$, on four qubits, and let $J\in\{0,1,2\}$. Every traceless anti-Hermitian operator $X$ on four qubits that commutes with $S^{[4]}_x,S^{[4]}_y,S^{[4]}_z$ and annihilates the orthogonal complement of the total-spin-$J$ subspace lies in $L$.
--
--   For $J=0,1,2$ the degeneracies are $n_J=2,3,1$, and these $X$ are exactly $\mathfrak{su}(2)\otimes I_1$, $\mathfrak{su}(3)\otimes I_3$ and $\mathfrak{su}(1)\otimes I_5=0$, each extended by zero: the exchange interaction acts as the full $\mathfrak{su}(n_J)$ on each of $\mathrm{DFS}_4(0)$, $\mathrm{DFS}_4(1)$, $\mathrm{DFS}_4(2)$, independently of the others. This is the base case $n=4$ of the induction of Appendix C.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The explicit operators (C.5)–(C.6) of the page are not part of the statement.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: "we can independently perform su(1) (i.e. zero), su(3), and su(2)" → for each J ∈ {0, 1, 2}, every element of su(n_J) ⊗ I_{2J+1} on the total-spin-J block, extended by zero, lies in L; reason: an operator commuting with the collective spins and supported on the block is Y ⊗ I_{2J+1} with tr X = (2J+1) tr Y, so these are exactly the hypotheses on X
-- source:
--   Bacon, Decoherence, Control, and Symmetry in Quantum Computers, arXiv:quant-ph/0305025v1, §C.1, pp.246–247 (PDF pp.263–264), with (C.5)–(C.6)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_ExchangeUniv_collS
import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_ExchangeUniv_exchangeLie
import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_ExchangeUniv_dfsSpace
import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_ExchangeUniv_suOn

open scoped Matrix

attribute [local instance 100] LieRing.ofAssociativeRing

namespace BaconThesis.ExchangeUniv

/-- **§C.1, `n = 4`: BaconThesis.Collective.exchange performs `su(1)`, `su(3)` and `su(2)` independently.**
Bacon, *Decoherence, Control, and Symmetry in Quantum Computers*, arXiv:quant-ph/0305025v1, §C.1, pp.246–247 (PDF pp.263–264): "For n = 4 the Hilbert space splits up into one
J = 2-irrep [DFS₄(2)], three J = 1-irreps [DFS₄(1)], and two J = 0-irreps [DFS₄(0)] … Direct
calculation of the effect of BaconThesis.Collective.exchange on these DFSs shows that we can independently perform su(1)
(i.e. zero), su(3), and su(2)", with the explicit operators (C.5)–(C.6).

For `J ∈ {0, 1, 2}`, every traceless anti-Hermitian `X` on four qubits that commutes with
`S_x, S_y, S_z` and acts only on the total-spin-`J` subspace (zero on its orthogonal complement)
lies in the BaconThesis.Collective.exchange Lie algebra `L` of four qubits. For `J = 0, 1, 2` these `X` are exactly
`su(2) ⊗ I₁`, `su(3) ⊗ I₃` and `su(1) ⊗ I₅ = 0`, extended by zero.

**Formalization Note.** `L = exchangeLie 4`; the explicit generators (C.5)–(C.6) are not part of
the statement (they are a route to its proof). -/
theorem n4_independent (J : ℝ) (hJ : J = 0 ∨ J = 1 ∨ J = 2)
    (X : Matrix (Fin 4 → Fin 2) (Fin 4 → Fin 2) ℂ) (hX : X ∈ suOn (dfsSpace 4 J))
    (hcomm : ∀ α : Fin 3, Commute X (collS 4 α)) :
    X ∈ exchangeLie 4 := by sorry

end BaconThesis.ExchangeUniv
